<section vertical-align="center">
  <grid spacing="none">
    <grid-item>
      <p role="eyebrow">Final position</p>
      <h2>Three bearings bring us home</h2>
      <p><b>Keep these bearings together.</b> Each describes the same cosmic history from a different angle.</p>
      <grid padding-top="42pt" spacing="medium" marker-straddle="true" marker-align="center" marker-position="above" marker-shape="circle" marker-style="solid" marker-size="large" marker="icon">
        <grid-item colspan="20" cell-style="subtle">
          <grid-item-label><ai-icon query="expanding coordinate grid"></ai-icon></grid-item-label>
          <h3>Expansion</h3>
          <p>Space stretches; distances grow. The change is not motion away from one cosmic port.</p>
        </grid-item>
        <grid-item colspan="20" cell-style="subtle">
          <grid-item-label><ai-icon query="cooling temperature instrument"></ai-icon></grid-item-label>
          <h3>Cooling</h3>
          <p>Changing conditions allow nuclei, atoms, stars, and galaxies to form in sequence.</p>
        </grid-item>
        <grid-item colspan="20" cell-style="subtle">
          <grid-item-label><ai-icon query="spectral wave and microwave signal"></ai-icon></grid-item-label>
          <h3>Evidence</h3>
          <p>Cosmological redshift and relic microwave light fit that same expanding history.</p>
        </grid-item>
      </grid>
      <spacer mode="fixed" size="18pt"></spacer>
      <grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug">
        <grid-item background-color="accent" padding="14pt 18pt">
          <p font-size="lg"><b>If asked where it happened, answer:</b> not at one cosmic port—the change belongs to the universe itself.</p>
        </grid-item>
      </grid>
    </grid-item>
  </grid>
</section>